> 
> ---
> 
> The Finnish Prime Minister’s Office has started conducting annual societal sustainability assessments. These assessments scope research results and knowledge gaps on ecosystem-related risks to the Finnish economy and society in the short to medium term, in addition to other various sustainability challenges. With leadership from the Prime Minister’s Office, Finland's new fifty million euro EU-funded LIFE Priodiversity project focuses on biodiversity policy coherence among various ministries, including the Ministry of Finance (Finnish Ministry of Finance, 2024).
> 
> **Tools and Approaches to Identify and Quantify Potential Adaptation Solutions to Manage Physical Risks**
> 
> Adaptation has not yet been integrated into macroeconomic assessments of physical climate risks. Many models and tools described in this document exclude adaptation or assume perfect adaptation levels, neglecting the interaction between varying adaptation levels and economic impacts. This is an evolving area requiring the combination of long-established approaches, such as integrated assessment models, cost-benefit analysis, and catastrophe models, with more recent and innovative analytics. Much of the analysis to date has emerged from the field of disaster risk management, now feeding into the emerging field of the economics of adaptation. Recent disasters have exposed the inadequacies of current risk management practices in both high- and low-income countries, highlighting how current approaches to resilience are inadequate to cope with today’s levels of risk, let alone keep pace with rapidly escalating threats (Marsh McLennan, 2023).
> 
> Examples of macroeconomic impact assessments of physical risks with and without adaptation that have been conducted as part of national climate change risk and adaptation assessments include the World Bank’s Economics of Adaptation to Climate Change methodology, developed between two thousand eight and two thousand ten. This methodology helps countries assess economy-wide climate impacts and identify adaptation responses using a computable general equilibrium model. Recently, these assessments have been supported by the open-source CLIMADA tool. CLIMADA is an analytical tool for assessing physical risks and developing climate adaptation strategies, supporting decision-making through a probabilistic risk modeling approach to simulate the economic impacts of extreme weather events. It aids policymakers in identifying cost-effective adaptation measures and facilitates financial planning for ministries of finance. The United Nations University and the Munich Climate Insurance Initiative have supported ministries in Vietnam, Honduras, and Ethiopia with CLIMADA for climate adaptation studies. In two thousand twenty-two, the Global Resilience Partnership demonstrated flood management benefits in Ghana using CLIMADA and is analyzing tropical cyclone risk in Madagascar. In Niger, CLIMADA assessed drought impacts, revealing that ecosystem-based adaptation investments could save nine point seven billion United States dollars in humanitarian costs.
> 
> Another example is the European Union’s PESETA project, which stands for Projection of Economic Impacts of Climate Change in Sectors of the European Union. This project uses integrated assessment models and sector-specific economic models to evaluate the impacts of climate change on different sectors of the European economy. It combines climate projections with economic and environmental models to assess potential damages across sectors such as agriculture, health, energy, and infrastructure. The project uses damage functions and cost-benefit analysis to quantify the economic consequences of climate change under various emission scenarios and adaptation strategies. Additionally, the project provided physical and monetary metrics to inform the EU Strategy on Adaptation to Climate Change in two thousand twenty-one (Watkiss and Hunt, 2012). 
> 
> Moreover, drawing on high-resolution climate data with sectoral impact and economic models, the American Climate Prospectus project incorporated market-driven adaptation. This was achieved through its computable general equilibrium model, which captures the effects of direct impacts on linked markets through price changes. However, while the project’s empirical analyses were specifically employed to reflect existing endogenous adaptation to weather events, it failed to capture any policy-driven adaptation (Ciscar et al., 2019).
